   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the general obligations law, in relation to excluding certain\nseasonal use tenancies from the provisions regulating deposits and advances\nfor residential dwelling units",
    "basePrintNo": "S6877",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6877-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 5,
       "fullName": "Jim Gaughran",
       "imgName": "1042_Jim_Gaughran.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1222,
       "sessionMemberId": 1807,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "GAUGHRAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §7-108, Gen Ob L",
    "lawSection": "General Obligations Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6877      REVISED 05/20/2021\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general obligations law, in relation to excluding\ncertain seasonal use tenancies from the provisions regulating deposits\nand advances for residential dwelling units\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo clarify that certain seasonal use tenancies are excluded from the\nprovisions of the General Obligations Law regulating the amount of a\ndeposit or advance that may be required for residential dwelling units.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends subdivision 1-a of Section 7-108 of the\ngeneral obligations law by amending paragraph a of subdivision 1 and\nadding new subdivisions 4 and 5 to exclude certain seasonal dwelling\nunits from the provisions regulating the amount of a deposit or advance\nthat may be required for residential dwelling units. The exclusion\napplies where the lease expressly provides that the dwelling unit is\nregistered as a seasonal use welling unit, the occupancy of the tenant\nis only for seasonal use not to exceed 120 days, and such tenant has a\nprimary address to return to. The dwelling unit must register with the\nappropriate local government, county or state registry as a seasonal use\ndwelling. Such a dwelling unit cannot be rented as a seasonal use dwell-\ning for more than 120 days per year.\n\nSection 2 of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis legislation would make clear that the generally applicable limit on\nthe amount of security deposits and advances that renters may be\nrequired to pay does not apply to seasonal use dwelling units that meet\nstrict conditions. Larger deposits are common in many vacation homes and\nthe policy concerns that motivated the legislature to limit the amount\ntenants may be required to pay when moving into a new home are much less\nrelevant when the renter is intending to stay for a short period and has\na permanent home to return to.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2019: A8546 (Thiele) - Judiciary / S6689 (LaValle): Judiciary\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
